From f0137e0000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH 0137e] evergo: hand back the kernel proc's ro, not a zeroed one

0135e stopped proc_get_ro() from panicking on a NULL p_proc_ro by allocating a
replacement proc_ro. That only moved the crash: the replacement is all zeroes,
so proc_ucred() reads a NULL p_ucred out of it and

  kauth_cred_proc_ref():  cred = proc_ucred(procp); kauth_cred_ref(cred);

dereferences NULL - the v61 boot died in _kauth_cred_proc_ref+0x30 with
far 0x0 (esr 0x96000006).

The ro really is torn down before the proc is: proc_reap_internal() does

  child->p_proc_ro = proc_ro_release_proc(child->p_proc_ro);
  proc_ro_free(child->p_proc_ro);
  child->p_proc_ro = NULL;          /* while the proc struct is still alive */
  ...
  proc_wait_release(child);         /* the struct is freed later */

so a zombie's p_proc_ro is NULL for a while by design. Rather than fabricate a
ro, return kernproc's - the same fallback current_proc_ro() already uses. Every
field in it is valid (real ucred, pid 0, name "kernel_task"), so callers that
only read it to report on a dying proc get sane values instead of a fault.
---
diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c b/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c
index cf02129..8f5fbc3 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_proc.c
@@ -4947,22 +4947,6 @@ evergo_proc_ro_recover(task_t t)
 
 	return proc_ro_alloc(PROC_NULL, NULL, t, &data);
 }
-
-/*
- * evergo (0135e): give a proc back a usable proc_ro.
- *
- * proc_ro_proc(NULL) returns PROC_NULL since 0129e, so a proc whose
- * p_proc_ro was already destroyed no longer dies on a NULL deref; it dies
- * on the backref check in proc_get_ro() instead. Build a ro that owns this
- * proc and install it, so every later reader sees a consistent, zeroed ro.
- */
-proc_ro_t
-evergo_proc_ro_recover_proc(proc_t p)
-{
-	struct proc_ro_data data = {};
-
-	return proc_ro_alloc(p, &data, TASK_NULL, NULL);
-}
 #endif /* ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET */
 
 void
@@ -5048,21 +5032,20 @@ proc_get_ro(proc_t p)
 
 #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
 	/*
-	 * evergo (0135e): the proc outlived its proc_ro. Repair instead of
-	 * panicking: allocate a ro that names this proc, install it and hand
-	 * it back. Without the install every call would leak another element.
+	 * evergo (0137e): a zombie's p_proc_ro is NULL on purpose - see
+	 * proc_reap_internal(), which frees the ro and clears the pointer while
+	 * the proc struct is still reachable.
+	 *
+	 * Do not fabricate a replacement (0135e did, and the zeroed ro made
+	 * proc_ucred() return a NULL cred that kauth_cred_proc_ref() then
+	 * dereferenced). Hand back kernproc's ro instead: every field in it is
+	 * valid, and current_proc_ro() already uses the same fallback.
 	 */
 	if (__improbable(ro == NULL)) {
-		proc_ro_t fresh = evergo_proc_ro_recover_proc(p);
-
 		printf("SWEET PROC: proc %p (%s) had p_proc_ro == 0, caller %p - "
-		    "repaired with %p\n", p, &p->p_name[0],
-		    __builtin_return_address(0), fresh);
-		if (fresh == NULL) {
-			panic("SWEET PROC: proc_ro recovery failed for proc %p", p);
-		}
-		p->p_proc_ro = fresh;
-		return fresh;
+		    "using kernproc ro\n", p, &p->p_name[0],
+		    __builtin_return_address(0));
+		return kernproc->p_proc_ro;
 	}
 #endif
 
